## Included in this repository

### electrs

`docs/electrs-0.11.1-knots-29.4.2-rpc.patch` is a patch against electrs
(<https://github.com/romanz/electrs>, MIT licence, Copyright (C) 2018, Roman Zeyde) for a Knots
29.4.2 RPC. It holds our changed lines with the lines of electrs around them, and is under
electrs' MIT licence. The header format and staged hash in `agentwallet/headers.py` are a port
of `headerv2.rs`, a file of our own header-v2 patch to electrs 0.11.1
(`patches/electrs-0.11.1-header-v2.patch` in <https://github.com/AwokenLazarus/Bitcoin>, MIT).

### Bitcoin Knots: rules ported into the Python

The difficulty and header rules in `agentwallet/headers.py` are our port of Bitcoin Knots'
(`src/pow.cpp` `GetNextWorkRequired`, `CalculateNextWorkRequired`,
`PermittedDifficultyTransition`; the checkpoint, assumed-valid block and minimum chain work of
its chain parameters). Knots (<https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin>, maintained by Luke
Dashjr, whose BLAKE2b proof of work this is) is under the MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2009-2025
The Bitcoin Core developers, Copyright (c) 2009-2025 Bitcoin Developers. `agentwallet/fwd/tx.py`
ports Knots' unified opt-in signature hash (Kyle Santiago).

### MuSig2

`agentwallet/fwd/musig2.py` is a pure-Python test oracle that follows the algorithms of BIP 327
(MuSig2) by name. The credit for the scheme is the BIP's authors'.

### libsecp256k1

Channel signatures are verified through the `xbt402` reference's `coincurve` backend, which builds
libsecp256k1 (<https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1>, MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2013 Pieter
Wuille).

### Bitcoin Knots test vectors

`tests/vectors/` holds header vectors captured from a Bitcoin Knots BLAKE2b regtest node and from
XBT mainnet through a Knots node, used to test the header code. They are recorded chain data.

## Python libraries

The tests install the packages in `requirements.txt`: mcp (MIT), anyio (MIT) and cryptography
(Apache-2.0 or BSD-3-Clause), and import the `xbt402` reference. They are not vendored. Their licence texts must accompany any distribution
that includes them.
